  "query_preview": "..\n\nDissipating right jet exit region over southern Lower Michigan is \nstill facilitating upper level convergence, leading to a weak \nsubsidence tendency. This occurs as longwave troughing remains \nentrenched across most of the Great Lakes region for the next 24 \nhours, with a VortMax sweeping across northeast Lower and northern \nLake Huron. Forecast soundings indicate a seasonably dry column with \nthe 22.18Z KDTX RAOB measuring a PWAT near 0.55 inches, below the \nclimatological 10th percentile. Diurnal cumulus positioned just \nbelow a 5 kft inversion layer wanes this evening, after the boundary \nlayer decouples. Expect mostly clear skies overnight as surface high \npressure builds southeastward. Winds trend toward calm after \nmidnight as the gradient relaxes.\n\nThe surface ridge centers over Chicago on Thursday with the longwave \naxis departing east. This results in mid level flow turning zonal \namidst geopotential height rises. 850 mb temperatures are forecast \nto moderate, given the anticyclonic orientation to the lower column \nflow. Warm advection setup lifts values to around 11C by Thursday \nafternoon, marking a steady warming trend that should progress \nthrough the weekend. Favorable insolation conditions arise given a \nlow cloud fraction and weak winds, lifting highs into the upper 70s.\n\nThe current stretch of dry weather persists Friday as the center of \nthe anticyclone drifts east, settling over the Ohio Valley. This \ncauses surface flow to back toward the south-southeast, initiating \nthe gradual rise in dewpoints over the weekend. Winds aloft begin to \nstrengthen Friday night and into Saturday as the synoptic pattern \nbecomes more active. A large ridge over the western half of CONUS \namplifies with stronger embedded flow rounding the apex. This \ninterfaces with a deep upper low over Hudson Bay. Expect the \nresident dry air to delay saturation until late Saturday or early \nSunday, ahead of an ejecting ridge-running shortwave targeting \nSoutheast Michigan. Showers and storms are possible with this wave \nas moisture/instability increase. Sunday or Monday appears to be the \nwarmest day of the forecast period, with highs briefly breaking \nabove normal, into the upper 80s.",
